---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A baker's bench reduced to flat gold and black shapes in a two-ink risograph print: a scored round loaf, a coiled banneton, a scale with a ball of dough on it, two crossed baguettes, a peel leaning at the far end, and three tin loaves on a shelf behind"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Most weeks you carry in something you fermented at home — a poolish (flour
    and water left to ferment overnight with a trace of yeast), a levain (a
    spoonful of sourdough starter built up the day before), a whole dough out
    of the fridge — and the Bake itself is shaping, baking and arguing about
    the result. You weigh in
    grams, write every formula in bakers' percentages, take the temperature of
    the dough instead of guessing at it, and keep a log of timings and room
    temperature, so that when the croissants leak butter onto the tray in week
    10 you can name the decision that caused it. Each Bake has a lecture to read
    first: scoring and steam before the baguettes, the fold schedule before the
    ciabatta. Almost every loaf is cut open and tasted before you leave. The rye
    is not allowed near a knife for two days, and the brioche goes home raw in
    your fridge.
  </p>
  <p>
    The order is deliberate. Lean doughs first, then the wet ones, then a
    starter you feed yourself, then a bagel dough stiff enough to fight back,
    then butter and eggs, then lamination, then a banana bread raised with soda,
    and last a flatbread cooked in seconds on a hot surface rather than in the
    air of an oven. Three bake-offs carry the marks: lean doughs in week 4
    (20%), fermentation in week 8 (30%), and a showpiece you propose at Bake 11
    and bake a fortnight after teaching ends (50%). Each is judged on a loaf the
    markers cut in front of you and on the notes that produced it, never on a
    photograph. You leave with twelve Bakes logged and formulas in percentages,
    which means you can scale any of them to the tin you actually own.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    A level-3 course that assumes you can cook and assumes nothing about your
    baking. If you can follow a recipe and read a thermometer you have enough to
    start, and week 1 begins with flour, water, salt and yeast in a tin. If you
    already bake sourdough most weekends, weeks 1 to 3 will be revision and
    weeks 9 to 12 will not be.
  </p>
  <p>
    You need a kitchen of your own as well, because dough does not wait for a
    timetable. The kit is a digital scale that reads to 1 gram, a plastic
    scraper, an instant-read probe, a 900 g loaf tin, a lidded cast-iron pot big
    enough to hold a 900 g loaf, and fridge space for an overnight retard — the
    dough finishing its rise in the cold, not on the counter. A banneton (a
    cloth-lined basket that holds a soft dough's shape while it proofs) and a
    lame (a slim blade for scoring, not a knife) join the list at the
    sourdough in week 6. A domestic
    oven is enough, as long as it will hold 250 °C, because every bake-off loaf
    is baked in your own oven and carried in finished. Wheat is unavoidable
    here, and the marking involves eating what you baked. If gluten is a problem
    for you, so is this course.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The week-by-week schedule and what happens in each Bake.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessments" href="/assessments/">
      <p>The three bake-offs: what each asks for, and how it is marked.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Marisol Quaye and Idris Fenn, who runs what, and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
